description:
Build `OrderedSet`, a binary search tree of distinct `int`s whose nodes also store the **size** of their subtree:

```cpp
class OrderedSet
{
public:
    bool insert(int key);                  // false if already there
    bool erase(int key);                   // false if absent
    bool contains(int key) const;
    std::size_t size() const;
    std::optional<int> min() const;
    std::optional<int> max() const;
    std::optional<int> floor(int x) const;     // largest key <= x
    std::optional<int> ceiling(int x) const;   // smallest key >= x
    std::size_t rank(int x) const;             // how many keys are < x
    int select(std::size_t k) const;           // the k-th smallest (from 0); std::out_of_range if k >= size()
    std::size_t countBetween(int lo, int hi) const;   // keys in [lo, hi]; 0 if lo > hi
    std::vector<int> toVector() const;          // in order
};
```

- `rank`, `select` and `countBetween` must be O(height), using the subtree sizes: no walking over every key. (`countBetween` is `rank(hi + 1) - rank(lo)`, with care at `INT_MAX`.)
- Keep the sizes correct through `insert` and `erase`, including erasing a node with two children.
- The tree does not need to rebalance; the tests insert keys in a shuffled order.

